@meta
id: 21-03
title: Twenty at once
chapter: 21
type: lesson
skills: macro-count, macro-record
par: 74
minutes: 7
vscode: a regex replace with three groups, and a header row you forgot
open: orders.fixture.ts

@brief
The orders fixture arrived as CSV, header row and all, pasted into the
middle of an array. Each data row becomes a record:

    ord_101,cus_7,1200        →    { id: 'ord_101', customerId: 'cus_7', total: 1200 },

Twenty rows. The header row is not data; it goes.

A substitute could do this with three capture groups, if you get the
pattern right first time. The macro is the edit you would make by hand
on one line, with three `f,` in it, and then a number in front of `@a`.

@keys
| `20@a` | play `a` twenty times |
| `f,` | to the next comma |
| `s` | replace the character under the cursor and start inserting |

@hints
Delete the header first, so the cursor is on the first real row when
you start recording.
--
On one line: `^i{ id: '` `Esc`, then `f,` and `s', customerId: '` `Esc`,
then `f,` and `s', total: ` `Esc`, then `A },` `Esc` and `j`. The `s`
on the comma is the trick: it deletes the comma and starts typing in
the same keystroke.
--
`jdd`, record it, then `19@a`. Nineteen, because the recording already
did the first row.

@solution
    j dd   qa ^i{ id: ' <Esc> f, s', customerId: ' <Esc> f, s', total:  <Esc> A }, <Esc> j q   19@a   :wq<Enter>

Sixty-one keystrokes, and the count is three of them.

A count in front of `@a` is exactly a count in front of anything else:
do it that many times. Nineteen replays of the macro, each starting
where the last one's `j` left off. Twenty rows or two hundred, the
count is the only thing that changes.

Notice that counting the rows was not really necessary. If you had said
`25@a` the macro would have run off the bottom of the data and stopped
on its own. The next lessons are about why that is safe and how to
lean on it.

@start orders.fixture.ts
